{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310802,"nonce":"108UA5V1qY01q6glrKHU5vmDQfQ=","title":"MEASURED+FIXED: the CLI auto-defines a flag per loaded plugin, which moved the stdlib cache key off the carried snapshot — 4x cold start ...","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-18 on a hello-world that uses NEITHER plugin.**\n\n| | cold start | stdlib cache |\n|---|---|---|\n| no plugins | 77.6 ms | `cache_hit=42 miss=0` |\n| 2 plugins (before) | **312.3 ms** | `cache_hit=0 miss=51` |\n| 2 plugins (after fix) | **83.3 ms** | `cache_hit=42 miss=0` |\n\n**dlopen is NOT the cost — it is ~2 ms.** The question \"does dlopen affect cold\nstart\" has been asked twice; the answer is no. The 235 ms was the standard\nlibrary being lowered from source all over again.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE.** `src/main.rs` (~1709) auto-defines a flag per loaded plugin so\nlibrary code can write `#if gpu`. Those defines land in `extra_defines`, which\nfeeds `cache_discriminator()`, which is the first path component of every\nsnapshot key (`\u003cdiscriminator\u003e/\u003cmodule\u003e.blade`). The snapshot is generated with\nno plugins, so a program loading `librayzor_tensors` + `libnue_plugins` asks for\n`native-6604088a/...` when the archive only holds `native-dc3bd338/...` — a\ntotal miss on all 42 modules. Nothing is *rejected*, so `RAYZOR_DEBUG_BLADE`\nprints nothing: the key simply is not there. **A silent lookup miss and a hash\nrejection look completely different in that log — absence of `[blade]` lines\nmeans the KEY is wrong, not the content.**\n\n**THE FIX (this session).** A define reaches a module only through\n`#if`/`#elseif`, so one the library never names cannot change what it lowers to.\n`compiler/build.rs` now scans `haxe-std` for the flags its own sources test and\nemits `STDLIB_OBSERVED_DEFINES` (36 of them); the library's cache directory,\nsnapshot key and source hash all count only those (`stdlib_relevant_defines()`).\nVerified 184/184 haxe tests, clippy clean.\n\n**TWO TRAPS IN THAT SCAN:**\n- Haxe allows guarded code on the same line (`#if cpp trace(\"x\"); #end`), so a\n  naive \"rest of line\" split harvests comment words — the first attempt produced\n  `will`, `github`, `join`, `issues`. Parse only the condition: one identifier,\n  or a balanced parenthesised group.\n- `llvm` and `jit` appear in haxe-std ONLY inside doc comments, so they are\n  correctly excluded — which means the jit and llvm tiers now share one stdlib\n  artifact and the snapshot went 126 entries/772 KB → **84 entries/514 KB**.\n  That is right, not a loss.\n- `wasm` is named by NO stdlib `#if` either, but the compiler branches on it\n  when it lowers (host-import vs native bindings). It is kept explicitly, or\n  wasm and native artifacts would collide — the exact bug the original\n  discriminator comment warns about.\n\n**IT EXPOSES A LATENT BUG — read [[bugs_restored_modules_lose_the_llvm_tier]]\nBEFORE shipping this.** Making the snapshot hit means the library is now\nRESTORED on run 1 of a plugin program instead of compiled, and restore installs\ntrap stubs that cost the LLVM tier. Cold llama-chat went 0 traps → 3. Runs 2+\nwere already in that state, so it is not a new defect, but it does move nue's\nfirst run onto Cranelift. The restore fix removes the trade-off entirely.\n\n**MEASUREMENT SETUP:** `rm -rf .rayzor` between runs, and DISCARD the first run\nafter any build (page-cache artifact: 80 ms vs 31 ms steady). The snapshot goes\nstale on any `compiler/src` change — converge with build → `cargo run --release\n-p snapshot-gen` → build. A wiped `target/` takes the snapshot with it, and cold\nstart silently reverts to ~265 ms; `build.rs` warns, so read the build output.\n\n\n**FOLLOW-UP BUG THIS CHANGE CAUSED, FIXED `eceff474`.** Once a build carried a\nVALID snapshot, `snapshot-gen` restored the library from it instead of compiling\nit — and a restored module writes no artifact, so the generator collected\nnothing and refused with \"no standard library artifacts were produced\". The\nsnapshot became un-regenerable for as long as it stayed valid, which only shows\nup when you rebuild WITHOUT touching `compiler/src` (touching it changes\nBUILD_ID and masks the bug by invalidating the snapshot).\n\nFix: `RAYZOR_IGNORE_EMBEDDED_SNAPSHOT=1`, set by the generator. That env var is\nalso the way to make any run compile the library from source — useful for\nproving whether a failure comes from RESTORE or from compilation.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_plugin_defines_invalidate_the_stdlib_snapshot` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}